  "selected": {
    "code": "import sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    s = data[1].strip()\n    t = data[2].strip()\n\n    req = {}\n    for i in range(n):\n        x = s[i]\n        y = t[i]\n        if x not in req:\n            req[x] = set()\n        req[x].add(y)\n    for x, ys in req.items():\n        if len(ys) > 1:\n            print(-1)\n            return\n\n    mapping = {x: next(iter(ys)) for x, ys in req.items()}\n\n    # If some source maps to a target that itself is a source in S, that's fine; each mapping is separate.\n    # But need ensure no two sources have same target? That's fine; each needs its own operation.\n    # Check that final transformation can be achieved: applying each mapping once (in appropriate order) should yield T.\n    # Actually order matters if source equals target of another mapping. We compute by direct substitution counts.\n    # Since S letters may equal target letters, we need a valid sequence of global operations.\n    # Minimal operations is number of distinct required source letters (x != target).\n    # But conflicts: if source x maps to y, and y maps to z, applying x->y then y->z works. Good.\n    count = 0\n    for x, y in mapping.items():\n        if x != y:\n            count += 1\n    # Verify possible: apply operations in any order? Need ensure after operations, S becomes T.\n    # Let's simulate greedy: we need each x changed to its target. If y is another source and later changed, then x's target would be changed too, violating T.\n    # Need a valid sequence. Determine if mapping is consistent as a function: for every S letter x, target y. For y (target) if y is also in S and y has target z, then after changing x->y, y may later become z, so x's final would be z, not y -> invalid.\n    # Therefore mapping must be acyclic-ish with no source whose target is a source with different target.\n    # For every x in mapping, y = mapping[x]; if y in mapping and y != x, then applying x->y will make y occurrences (including from x) later become mapping[y]. Then x's final would be mapping[y], not y, so invalid unless mapping[y] == y.\n    invalid = False\n    for x, y in mapping.items():\n        if y in mapping:\n            if mapping[y] != y:\n                invalid = True\n                break\n    # But if y is in mapping and mapping[y] == y, then y remains y, fine.\n    if invalid:\n        # Could still be valid if x->y happens after y's mapping? But y's mapping global affects x too after x->y, regardless of order if x->y then y->z. If y->z then x->y, y->z already changed y but x still x, then x->y makes x into y, final y not z, so invalid. So invalid.\n        print(-1)\n        return\n\n    print(count)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()",
